# R11: Mayor Somerton comes out to greet Lord Phillip once the Round is quiet. Started by the call on the notice board (wm_notice_board) after wm_first_sacrifice_done.
# He asks pardon for the interruption, praises or forgives the party by what happened to the goat, offers the village, and then Phillip takes his leave.

~ start
appear wendel_somerton
Narrator: A big timber-framed house on the square opens its door, and a round man in a three-piece suit that strains at every button bustles out across the dirt, a cane in one hand and a bowler in the other.
wendel_somerton [smile]: Lord Phillip! Lady Adela! And what a way to be greeted. You have caught us in the middle of a very old custom, I'm afraid, and I do beg pardon for the commotion.
phillip_druskenvald: Mayor. Mr. Somerton, is it? We were told your village was quiet.
wendel_somerton: Quiet, my lord, quite, but for tonight. The oak drinks from the herds, once a year, so the hunting stays good and the barns stay full. A sacred tradition, nothing more.
if flag.goat_fate == "eaten"
Narrator: He mops his bald head with a handkerchief and looks, for the first time, at the strangers who stood by and let the thing happen.
wendel_somerton [smile]: And you, good travelers, were kind enough to let the rite alone. The Green Queen takes note of respect, and I shall see that the village does too.
-> welcome_after
endif
if flag.goat_fate == "caught"
wendel_somerton: Saved the little fellow, did you? Well, well. The oak will drink from the beasts of the field whatever you do, and a goat is a goat. We'll say no more of it.
Narrator: His smile holds, but his handkerchief has gone to his forehead, and he does not stop mopping.
-> welcome_after
endif
wendel_somerton: Cut it loose, did you? The rite is the village's, you understand, and the Queen does not forgive a spoiled offering as easily as I do. I shall say a word to the neighbors.
-> welcome_after

~ welcome_after
quest wickermoor_arrival welcome
* Ask him what the custom is for -> ask_custom
* [Insight DC 17] Watch him: he is more rattled than he lets on -> insight_ok | insight_fail
* Ask about the village, and where to sleep -> offer
* Say nothing more -> offer

~ ask_custom
wendel_somerton: A blood offering to the Green Queen, who keeps the Hollow's herds and hunts. Tradition, tradition. We've done it since before the clocktower, and the harvests have never once failed us.
-> welcome_after

~ insight_ok
Narrator: His eyes slide to the east road and back, and a muscle jumps in his jaw. He is a man hiding something to please a lord.
set mayor_admitted_troubles
wendel_somerton: Er. Some of our people have been, ah, taking sick lately. And a few have gone off without a word. Nothing to trouble his lordship with.
-> welcome_after

~ insight_fail
Narrator: He looks like exactly what he is, a mayor with a very good suit and a very full evening.
-> welcome_after

~ offer
wendel_somerton: The Druskenvald estate is quite ready for you, my lord. Pristine, staffed, a fine gift from the village. And for your friends, the Green Queen Inn, the one decent lodging in the Hollow. Alda Farnum keeps it.
* [Persuasion DC 15] Ask him plainly what is going wrong in the village -> press_ok | press_fail
* [Intimidation DC 15] Tell him we saw what that thing in the square was, and we want the truth -> press_ok | press_fail
* Thank him and let it go -> parting

~ press_ok
Narrator: The mayor's smile goes thin, and then goes altogether, and under it is a tired man in a tight suit.
wendel_somerton: More disappearances, more fevers, more funerals than there ought to be. I'd rather not say more in front of the lord. Come to my office when it is open, and I will tell you what I know.
set mayor_admitted_troubles
-> parting

~ press_fail
wendel_somerton: Oh, I'm sure I don't know what you can mean. A village is a village, good people. It has its seasons.
-> parting

~ parting
phillip_druskenvald [weary]: Mayor, I do hope you'll forgive us. It has been a long road, and I find I am dreadfully tired. Darling, shall we go up and see this gift of yours?
adela_druskenvald: Oh, swell. Philly, don't pout, the house will be darling. Friends, you'll come to dinner tomorrow, won't you, once we've seen the rooms?
* [Insight DC 15] Something is wrong with Phillip, and it is not just the road -> uneasy_ok | uneasy_fail
* Say we'd be glad to come -> accept

~ uneasy_ok
Narrator: Phillip's smile has been put on a half-beat late, and the hand on his cane is white at the knuckles. He is looking at the oak.
set phillip_uneasy_noticed
-> accept

~ uneasy_fail
-> accept

~ accept
phillip_druskenvald [smile]: Splendid. Forty gold between you for the inn, on me. The estate, tomorrow evening, and we shall have a grand old time.
gold +40
quest wickermoor_arrival parting
Narrator: You watch two small figures, one twirling a cane, walk up the winding path toward the crooked manor on its hill, the windows lit and every line askew.
leave phillip_druskenvald
leave adela_druskenvald
vanish wendel_somerton
set wm_welcomed
Narrator: In the round below, the oak's carved faces lie in the moonlight, and one of them, you would swear, turns to watch them go.
